<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mpezeni's Ngoni Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1865 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ngoni kingdom of Mpezeni in eastern Zambia, conquered by the British South Africa Company in 1898.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a> around 1848, his son Mpezeni led one branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> through the lands west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>. After years of migration and warfare, in the 1860s they settled on the plateau of what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chipata">Chipata</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mpezeni ruled a regimental society in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> pattern, drawing tribute and captives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsenga">Nsenga</a> and others. His people were noted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herders, and his court received traders and concession hunters as European interest grew in the region's supposed gold.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disputes with a company holding a concession on his land led to war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> in 1898. Mpezeni was captured and his son Nsingu executed, and the territory became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-eastern-rhodesia">North-Eastern Rhodesia</a>, run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chipata">Fort Jameson</a>. His successors remain paramount chiefs.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1865 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.82%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1865 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mpezeni</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ngoni, Chinsenga</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Ngoni, Mpezeni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=mpezenis-ngoni-kingdom" aria-label="Mpezeni's Ngoni Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5391 2721 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mpezeni's Ngoni Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 2875h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5711 2977h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5711 2977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Zambia. <a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=mpezenis-ngoni-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

